{:edb/db-before-t 2353 :edb/db-after-t 2354 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535666 50 #inst "2026-10-14T20:30:40.981Z" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186054800 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525006 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186061579 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061583 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186064160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525043 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186064229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525050 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186064256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525035 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186064364 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524972 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186064564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186064564 1071 "$(-18)÷6=$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186064564 1071 "What is $(-18) \\div 6$?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186064564 1074 "Here, we are dividing a negative number $(-18)$ by a positive one $(6).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-18)÷6=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we divide $18$ by $6$ without any signs: $18÷6=3.$ Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n(-18)÷6=-3.\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186073739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524993 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186073880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525042 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186073899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525009 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089250 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089251 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089252 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089253 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089254 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524505 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524606 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524659 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524715 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186089249 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524836 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186089249 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524659 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186090472 1078 #edb/ref 17592186090476 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092118 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092118 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}ln⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186092261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524994 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524997 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524996 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524975 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525010 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525003 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525034 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524998 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525045 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525054 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524980 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524992 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525013 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092399 1050 900.0 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092513 1071 "Given that $f(x) = e^{2x + 5}$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186092513 1071 "Given that $f(x)={e}^{2x+5},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092513 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=2x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092712 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $8$, what is the **smallest possible** degree of $P - Q$?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186092712 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $8$, what is the **smallest possible** degree of $P-Q?$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186092712 1074 "The smallest possible degree of $P-Q$ is $0.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{8}$ and $Q={x}^{8}-2.$ Then $P-Q=2,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186093116 1061 2491 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186093116 1061 646582 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186093117 1061 2492 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186093117 1061 646583 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186093118 1061 2495 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186093118 1061 646589 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186093120 1061 2498 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186093120 1061 646586 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095479 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095480 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095481 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095482 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095483 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524427 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524499 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524674 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524807 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186095478 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524821 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186095478 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524674 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096400 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096400 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}{)}^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186096402 1074 "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096402 1074 "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2}{)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x{)}^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x{)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186096610 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524982 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096610 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525017 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525040 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524984 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525021 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524983 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096888 1050 1020.0 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186096892 1050 420.0 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186097259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186097259 1071 "Find $f^{′}(x)$ for $f(x) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 3x}}$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186097259 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3x}}.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186097259 1074 "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+3x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{′}=4x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (4x+3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186097807 1064 "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{'}(a) & ={lim}_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{'}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{'}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186097807 1064 "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & ={lim}_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186097815 1064 "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186097815 1064 "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186100811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524973 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186100920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524961 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186103064 1078 #edb/ref 17592186103067 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186105863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525038 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186106198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186106198 1071 "![](images/59/597626d534c99880de4eabaf51be1c756149395ce285e843a9eaa9a8bdc395d8.png)\n\nWhich vertices are adjacent to $E$?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186106198 1071 "![](images/59/597626d534c99880de4eabaf51be1c756149395ce285e843a9eaa9a8bdc395d8.png)\n\nWhich vertices are adjacent to $E?$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186106198 1074 "$E$ is the endpoint of the sides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and $\\overset{―}{EF}.$ Therefore, the vertices $D$ and $F$ are adjacent to $E.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186110675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524990 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186112045 1078 #edb/ref 17592186112046 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186112057 1078 #edb/ref 17592186112058 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115088 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x+2)\n$$\n\nis a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left*. So, every point on the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x+2)\n$$\n\nis two units *to the left* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/75/758899a1abd5298429059016a4127db61eda014e8c46e0539e9226c5641e5ceb.png)" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115088 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x+2)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left*. So, every point on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$ is two units *to the left* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/75/758899a1abd5298429059016a4127db61eda014e8c46e0539e9226c5641e5ceb.png)" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115089 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2,0)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on $y=f(x-7)$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115089 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2,0)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on $y=f(x-7).$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115089 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x-7)$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $7$ units to the *right*. So the $y$-coordinate remains unchanged while the $x$-coordinate increases by $7$.\n\nWe know that the point $(-2,0)$ lies on $y=f(x)$. Therefore, the point\n\n$$\n(-2+7,0) = (5,0)\n$$\n\nmust lie on $y=f(x-7)$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115089 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x-7)$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $7$ units to the *right*. So the $y$-coordinate remains unchanged while the $x$-coordinate increases by $7.$\n\nWe know that the point $(-2,0)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(-2+7,0) = (5,0)$ must lie on $y=f(x-7).$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115090 1074 "To graph\n\n$$\ny=f(x+1)-2\n$$\n\nwe translate the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the left and then $2$ units down.\n\n![](images/17/1744139728266d53c546e0b98452e042b8d099cebed12f5ee0a7c230257e9fa2.png)" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115090 1074 "To graph $y=f(x+1)-2,$ we translate the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the left and then $2$ units down.\n\n![](images/17/1744139728266d53c546e0b98452e042b8d099cebed12f5ee0a7c230257e9fa2.png)" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115103 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) .$ What point must lie on $y=f(x + 1)+5?$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115103 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$. What point must lie on $y=f(x + 1)+5$?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115103 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x+1)+5\n$$\n\nis a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *left* and $5$ units up. So the $y$-coordinate increases by $5$ and the $x$-coordinate decreases by $1$.\n\nWe know that the point $P(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x)$. Therefore, the point\n\n$$\n(1-1,0+5) = (0,5)\n$$\n\nmust lie on\n\n$$\ny=f(x+1)+5\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115103 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x+1)+5$  is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *left* and $5$ units up. So the $y$-coordinate increases by $5$ and the $x$-coordinate decreases by $1.$\n\nWe know that the point $P(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1-1,0+5) = (0,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x+1)+5.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525060 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524979 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525020 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525037 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524981 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525008 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115418 1050 420.0 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115506 1071 "![](images/55/5573133cd1a199d53254947f951999a10731d980faffcf75e25ac6c3e66cd861.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f(x - 1)$?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115506 1071 "![](images/55/5573133cd1a199d53254947f951999a10731d980faffcf75e25ac6c3e66cd861.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x-1)?$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115506 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x-1)$ is a horizontal translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *right*. So, every point on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$ is one unit *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/96/96e78d86fc113988887c0c35daf2ebf119f2f47be8d82822d439b2f47433c31d.png)" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115508 1071 "![](images/f3/f35440c0132f7e98617f2d700b60563d44b7e9cb3af3850f557d4b169cc08bb7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f(x - 3) + 2$?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186115508 1071 "![](images/f3/f35440c0132f7e98617f2d700b60563d44b7e9cb3af3850f557d4b169cc08bb7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x-3)+2?$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186115508 1074 "To graph $y=f(x-3)+2,$ we translate the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units to the right and then $2$ units up.\n\n![](images/3c/3cdbb6ef28bc47734820fadaef1e65061775b8c96724713f7f8de30e8a5d0d5e.png)" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186116503 1064 "In general, for $k>0$,\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x-k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *right*, while\n- the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *left*.\n\n**Watch out!** It may seem counterintuitive that subtracting $k$ moves the graph to the right, while adding $k$ moves the graph to the left. However, this is in fact true.\n\nTo see why, let's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. Let's use this graph to plot the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)\n\nLet's take some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ and find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor example, take the point $(3,-4)$. The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means that\n\n$$\nf(3)=-4\n$$\n\nThen if\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(x-2) = f({\\color{red}5}-2) = f(3) = -4\n$$\n\nwhich means that $({\\color{red}5},-4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ with the same $y$-value and a $x$-value that is $2$ units *greater*! Several more examples of points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x-2)[/math] \\\\ (3,-4) & (5,-4) \\\\ (1, 2) & (3, 2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (2, 1.5) \\\\ (-2, 0) & (0, 0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's plot both curves.\n\n![](images/ed/edae088984432dc7d0fff816a9e016ad221a3c897a78cf79efe6c784fa9176fe.png)\n\nWe see that every point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ is shifted $2$ units *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186116503 1064 "In general, for $k>0,$\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x-k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *right*, while\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *left*.\n\n**Watch out!** It may seem counterintuitive that subtracting $k$ moves the graph to the right, while adding $k$ moves the graph to the left. However, this is in fact true.\n\nTo see why, let's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. Let's use this graph to plot the graph of $y=f(x-2).$\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)\n\nLet's take some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ and find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(x-2).$ \n\nFor example, take the point $(3,-4).$ The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means that $f(3)=-4.$ Then if $x={\\color{red}5},$ we have \n\n$$\nf(x-2) = f({\\color{red}5}-2) = f(3) = -4,\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that $({\\color{red}5},-4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ \n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ with the same $y$-value and a $x$-value that is $2$ units *greater*! Several more examples of points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x-2)[/math]} \\\\ (3,-4) & (5,-4) \\\\ (1,2) & (3,2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (2,1.5) \\\\ (-2,0) & (0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's plot both curves.\n\n![](images/ed/edae088984432dc7d0fff816a9e016ad221a3c897a78cf79efe6c784fa9176fe.png)\n\nWe see that every point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ is shifted $2$ units *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x).$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186117659 1078 #edb/ref 17592186117662 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186119939 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524978 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186119954 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525065 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186119979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525048 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186120250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186120250 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5, 9,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x + 2y + 7z =-9$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186120250 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,9,-6)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $3x+2y+7z=-9.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186120250 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=2$ and $y=3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+7z & =-9 \\\\ 3(2)+2(3)+7z & =-9 \\\\ 7z & =-21 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(2,3,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,-3⟩-⟨5,9,-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-6,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨3,2,7⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-3)\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 7|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{7}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-9-12+21|}{\\sqrt{9+4+49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,0\\,|}{\\sqrt{62}} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $P$ lies on the plane." 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186124505 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525044 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524967 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186147595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525012 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186152125 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524970 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156976 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156977 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156978 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156979 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186156980 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524388 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524429 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524521 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524628 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186156975 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524759 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186156975 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524429 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157195 1074 "Let $X$ represent the duration of the radio program. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U[27,34]$, and we wish to compute $P(X < 30)$.\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U[27,34]$, we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{7},\\begin{vmatrix}27 \\le x \\le 34 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 30) &= \\int_{27}^{30}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{27}^{30}\\frac{1}{7}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x}{7}\\right|_{27}^{30} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30}{7} - \\frac{27}{7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186157195 1074 "Let $X$ represent the duration of the radio program. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U[27,34],$ and we wish to compute $P(X < 30).$\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/2a/2a615c021abbdd4009eb1debcda9b9c3076f6a9f087c6e1e6fd9454b9334eb17.svg)\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U[27,34],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/93/938214f94631d071b2bcd6b672e3b9b903918a88676da2cdc3b978cd94d891b2.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<30) & ={\\int }_{27}^{30}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{27}^{30}\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{7}|}_{27}^{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{7}-\\frac{27}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157196 1071 "A video game randomly generates an obstacle the player has to avoid. The obstacle's $x$-coordinate is uniformly distributed along a segment whose endpoints have $x$-coordinates of $-6$ and $12$. What is the mean $x$-coordinate of the obstacle?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186157196 1071 "A video game randomly generates an obstacle the player has to avoid. The obstacle's $x$-coordinate is uniformly distributed along a segment whose endpoints have $x$-coordinates of $-6$ and $12.$ What is the mean $x$-coordinate of the obstacle?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157196 1074 "Let $X$ represent the $x$-coordinate of a randomly generated obstacle. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U[-6,12]$, and we wish to compute $\\textrm E[X]$.\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U[-6,12]$, we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{-6+12}{2} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean $x$-coordinate is $3$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186157196 1074 "Let $X$ represent the $x$-coordinate of a randomly generated obstacle. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U[-6,12],$ and we wish to compute $\\textrm E[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U[-6,12],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{-6+12}{2} = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean $x$-coordinate is $3.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157197 1074 "Let $X$ represent the visiting time of a randomly chosen visitor. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U[1.5,4.5]$, and we wish to compute $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U[a,b]$, then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U[1.5,4.5]$, we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(4.5-1.5)^2}{12} = \\dfrac{9}{12} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{hr}^2\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186157197 1074 "Let $X$ represent the visiting time of a randomly chosen visitor. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U[1.5,4.5],$ and we wish to compute $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U[1.5,4.5],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{(4.5-1.5)^2}{12} = \\dfrac{9}{12} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is $\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{hr}^2.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524959 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524995 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525018 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525052 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525056 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524968 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524969 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524977 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525022 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525049 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157377 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525004 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157377 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525027 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157377 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525029 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157377 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525066 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186157536 1050 420.0 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186158009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186158009 1071 "The time it takes a mechanic to repair a car is uniformly distributed with a minimum of $2$ hours and a maximum of $6$ hours. What is the variance of the time it takes to repair a car?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186158009 1071 "The time it takes a mechanic to repair a car is uniformly distributed with a minimum of $2$ hours and a maximum of $6$ hours. What is the variance of the time it takes to repair a car?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186158009 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time it takes to repair a car. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[2,6],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[2,6],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(6-2)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{16}{12}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the time it takes to repair a car is $\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{hr}}^{2}$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186158542 1064 "In general, if $X \\sim U[a,b]$ is uniformly distributed, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/93/93894f939fa0ef715ee6ab6c225f456972b746184673df0b8d8312656727d793.svg)\n\nWe can use a continuous uniform distribution to model situations where all possible outcomes on a finite interval are equally likely.\n\nFor instance, suppose a dental receptionist wants to model the waiting time of a randomly selected patient. After observing a number of patients over several days, the receptionist notices that the waiting time is uniformly distributed between $6$ and $14$ minutes, inclusive.\n\nThen, by letting $X$ be the amount of time a randomly selected patient spends waiting, we can model the waiting time using the continuous uniform distribution: \n\n$$\nX \\sim U[6,14]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/fd/fd37b3e19aad197ebb7eeabad00fca068455eed8da462a12630134cd7980941e.svg)\n\nWith this model, we can now make predictions about patient waiting times.\n\nFor example, the probability that a randomly selected patient waits longer than $9$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 9) & ={\\int }_{9}^{14}\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{9}^{14}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[x]}_{9}^{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[14-9] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also calculate the mean waiting time by finding the expected value of $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{a+b}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+14}{2} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean waiting time is $10$ minutes." 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186158542 1064 "In general, if $X \\sim U[a,b]$ is uniformly distributed, then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{b - a},\\begin{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWe can use a continuous uniform distribution to model situations where all possible outcomes on a finite interval are equally likely.\n\nFor instance, suppose a dental receptionist wants to model the waiting time of a randomly selected patient. After observing a number of patients over several days, the receptionist notices that the waiting time is uniformly distributed between $6$ and $14$ minutes, inclusive.\n\nThen, by letting $X$ be the amount of time a randomly selected patient spends waiting, we can model the waiting time using the continuous uniform distribution:\n\n$$\nX \\sim U[6,14]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{8},\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\le x \\le 14 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWith this model, we can now make predictions about patient waiting times.\n\nFor example, the probability that a randomly selected patient waits longer than $9$ minutes is equal to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 9) &= \\int_{9}^{14}\\frac{1}{8}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\\int_{9}^{14}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}[x]_{9}^{14} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}[14 - 9] \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also calculate the mean waiting time by finding the expected value of $X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= \\frac{a + b}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 + 14}{2} \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean waiting time is $10$ minutes." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186161108 1078 #edb/ref 17592186161113 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186162009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525014 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186162009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525025 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186162010 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524999 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186162636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186162636 1071 "Which of the following quadratic forms over $R^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\n1. $x^{T}Qx$, where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are $λ_{1} = 3$ and $λ_{2} = 5$\n2. $x^{T}Rx$, where the eigenvalues of $R$ are $λ_{1} = λ_{2} =-3$\n3. $x^{T}Sx$, where the eigenvalues of $S$ are $λ_{1} = 0$ and $λ_{2} =-1$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186162636 1071 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186162636 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ is positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186163774 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163776 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186163792 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163797 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186164469 1078 #edb/ref 17592186164471 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165477 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165478 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165479 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165480 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165481 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524469 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524550 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524582 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524762 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524790 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165476 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524582 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165483 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165484 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165485 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165486 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186165487 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524529 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524573 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186165482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524652 13194139535666 17592186