3 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186166877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525053 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186166877 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525062 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186166913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525064 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167116 1071 "The disjoint union $A_{1}⊔A_{2}⊔A_{3}$ of events $A_{1}, A_{2}, A_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P(A_{3} \\mid B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$P(B\\begin{vmatrix}A_{1}) = 0.5, \\\\ P(B \\mid A_{2}) = 0.6,\\end{vmatrix}P(A_{2}) = 0.2; P(B \\mid A_{3}) = 0.1, \\mid P(A_{3}) = 0.7$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186167116 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{3}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.5, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.6, & P({A}_{2})=0.2 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.1, & P({A}_{3})=0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167116 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=3,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{1}) & =1-(P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})) \\\\  & =1-(0.2+0.7) \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{3}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.1)(0.7)}{(0.5)(0.1)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.1)(0.7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.07}{0.24} \\\\  & ≈0.29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167119 1071 "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1$, mode $2$, and mode $3$. They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $45%$ of their muffins, and $80%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $35%$ of their muffins.\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $20%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $65%$ of all muffins were prepared with yeast A, $82%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, and $68%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned out well? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186167119 1071 "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1,$ mode $2,$ and mode $3.$ They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $45%$ of their muffins, and $80%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $35%$ of their muffins.\n\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $20%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $65%$ of all muffins were prepared with yeast A, $82%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, and $68%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned out well? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167119 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.8 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(G|{M}_{2}) \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.2 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.45+0.35+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $82%$ of muffins made with yeast A turned out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.82.\n$$\n\n- Since $68%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|G)=0.68.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned well, which is represented as $P(G|{M}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\ P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\ 0.68 & =\\frac{(0.82)(0.65)}{(0.8)(0.45)+P(G|{M}_{2})(0.35)+(0.9)(0.2)} \\\\ 0.68 & =\\frac{0.533}{0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(G|{M}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.68(0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54) & =0.533 \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54 & =\\frac{0.533}{0.68} \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54 & =\\frac{533}{680} \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2}) & =\\frac{533}{680}-0.54 \\\\ P(G|{M}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.35}(\\frac{533}{680}-0.54) \\\\ P(G|{M}_{2}) & ≈0.697\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167235 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167235 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t); y^{′}(t) =-4y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186167235 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/a2/a2196ea04e12f3c706300e0c9d15806606e408f5e582d3e4f2b9c62f0efc343f.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/14/1429eda4336d47aec06c7dd673ff6163203423739c520fd0c83db1c5cb072ac7.png)" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167378 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{x}^{4},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167378 1071 "If $y = 4\\ln x + arcsch x^{4}$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} =$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186167378 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}+(-\\frac{1}{|{x}^{4}|\\sqrt{1+({x}^{4})^{2}}})\\cdot ({x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{8}}}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}-\\frac{4}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{8}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{8}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167379 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arsech}⁡\\sqrt{x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186167379 1071 "If $y = arsech \\sqrt{x}$, then $\\frac{dy}{dx} =$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186167379 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}}}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{1-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186171243 1078 #edb/ref 17592186171248 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186172603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186172603 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6$?\n\n1. $14733189$\n2. $1589610$\n3. $873100584$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186172603 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $14\\,733\\,189$\n\nII. $1\\,589\\,610$\n\nIII. $873\\,100\\,584$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186172603 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $14\\,733\\,189$ is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- The number $1\\,589\\,610$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+5+8+9+6+1+0=30\n$$\n\nSo, $1\\,589\\,610$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $873\\,100\\,584$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n8+7+3+1+0+0+5+8+4=36\n$$\n\nSo, $873\\,100\\,584$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186173980 1078 #edb/ref 17592186173981 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186174304 1078 #edb/ref 17592186174308 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186174740 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524721 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186174740 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524721 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186175923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525046 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524960 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176033 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525051 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176038 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525028 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176038 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525032 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525023 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176039 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525058 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176287 1071 "Given that the random variables\n$X_{1}∼N(2, 1^{2}), X_{2}∼N(20, 5^{2}), X_{3}∼N(6, 5)$\nare mutually independent and $Y = 10X_{1} - 4X_{2} + 5X_{3}$, calculate $P(3 < Y < 40)$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$0.01$$0.02$$0.03$$1.2$ $0.8869$ $0.8888$ $0.8907$ $1.3$ $0.9049$ $0.9066$ $0.9082$ $1.4$ $0.9207$ $0.9222$ $0.9236$ |  | $z$$0.00$$0.01$$0.02$$2.7$ $0.9965$ $0.9966$ $0.9967$ $2.8$ $0.9974$ $0.9975$ $0.9976$ $2.9$ $0.9981$ $0.9982$ $0.9982$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186176287 1071 "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(2,{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(20,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,5)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent and $Y=10{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}+5{X}_{3},$ calculate $P(3<Y<40).$ \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.01$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.02$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.03$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.8869$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.8888$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.8907$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9049$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9066$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9082$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9207$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9222$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9236$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.00$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.01$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.02$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9965$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9966$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9967$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9974$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9975$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9976$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9981$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9982$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9982$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176287 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(2,{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(20,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(6,5),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=10{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}+5{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 20+5\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-30, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={10}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+{5}^{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =100\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 25+25\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(-30,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3<Y<40).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<Y<40) & =P(\\frac{3-(-30)}{\\sqrt{625}}<Z<\\frac{40-(-30)}{\\sqrt{625}}) \\\\  & =P(1.32<Z<2.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.8)-P(Z<1.32) \\\\  & =Φ(2.8)-Φ(1.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.32)≈0.9066,\\,Φ(2.8)≈0.9974.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<Y<40) & =Φ(2.8)-Φ(1.32) \\\\  & ≈0.9974-0.9066 \\\\  & ≈0.0908.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176341 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(7,-2).$ So, our actual value is $y=-2.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=7$ has the $y$-coordinate $-4.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=-4.$\n\n![](images/92/92a26b8ab019879dec3208878f6a0604c0616776b09e3f8fa916fcab72df3a73.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =-2-(-4) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176417 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-5)$ and is perpendicular to $x + 3y = 12$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186176417 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-5)$ and is perpendicular to $x+3y=12?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176417 1074 "First, we write the equation $x+3y=12$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =12 \\\\ 3y & =-x+12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+4.$ The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $3.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=3$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =3(x-1) \\\\ y+5 & =3x-3 \\\\ y & =3x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186179824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525001 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186185442 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185443 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186185490 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185493 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186185646 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185650 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524965 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524958 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188295 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524962 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525030 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525055 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524989 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525031 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525002 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525061 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525011 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188527 1071 "![](images/fe/fedd81f87921d865c375ecdbf9b83439092d138686e07753bb20ae522b6e49c9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 3, 3]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 3, 3]$ given that $f(0) = 56$." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186188527 1071 "![](images/fe/fedd81f87921d865c375ecdbf9b83439092d138686e07753bb20ae522b6e49c9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(0)=56.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188527 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ rectangles and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/96/962bea79c8308d1d63937aa15f19492f2dd0c31f278e2957a71255dbbd92a8ca.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =56 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56-(-2-2) \\\\  & =60 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56+2+2 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{56,60,60\\}=56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=56,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188535 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}).$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188535 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$f_{X}(x) = {2x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwisef_{Y}(y) = {3y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind $P(X \\le 0.5, Y \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}})$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186188535 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =6xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}6xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}6x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188561 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186188561 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$f_{X}(x) = {\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwisef_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{2}{3}y,\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind $P(0 \\le X \\le 0.4, Y \\le 2)$. Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186188561 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}y \\\\  & =y\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & ≈0.253.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186220816 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524678 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186220816 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524678 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186221444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524985 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186221445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524991 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186221456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524971 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186221456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525016 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186221457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524966 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186222023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186222023 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in [-3,3).$ The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{5}.$\n\n${b}_{5}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186222023 1071 "$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (\\frac{nπ}{3}x) + b_{n}\\sin (\\frac{nπ}{3}x)$\nLet $F(x) = 6x + 1$ for $x ∈ [- 3, 3)$. The Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of $b_{5}$.$b_{5} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186222023 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and $f(x)=6x+1$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{5}=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{3}(uv\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{3}{5\\pi }{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-{[(6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}+6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-((6(3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-(6(-3)+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\pi ))+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}x)]}_{-3}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(36+\\frac{18}{5\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186296661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524815 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186296661 1078 #edb/ref 17592186295565 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186296661 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524815 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186297290 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x}+12x+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({8}^{x})+12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8){8}^{x})+12(1)+0 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8){8}^{x}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186297290 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x}+12x+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({8}^{x})+12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3(ln⁡(8){8}^{x})+12(1)+0 \\\\  & =3ln⁡(8){8}^{x}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186314351 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524810 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186314351 1078 #edb/ref 17592186313486 13194139535666 17592186045417 false] [17592186314351 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524810 1319413